# CKA Drill: Exposing Deployments via NodePort Services (`my-web-app-service`)

This exercise covers the standard CKA exam objective: deploying a replicated web application (`wordpress`)
and exposing it across all cluster nodes using a dedicated Kubernetes `NodePort` service bound to a specific port.

---

## 1. Exam Objective & Requirements

1. Create a Deployment named `my-web-app-deployment` with:
   - Container image: `wordpress`
   - Replicas: `2`
   - Container port: `80`
2. Expose `my-web-app-deployment` as a Service named `my-web-app-service` with:
   - Service Type: `NodePort`
   - Port: `80`
   - NodePort: `30770`
3. Verify that the service is reachable on port `30770` on all cluster nodes (`192.168.56.20`, `.21`, `.22`).

---

## 2. In-Exam Rapid Workflow (Imperative)

In the CKA exam, speed is essential. Avoid writing entire manifests manually.

### Step 1: Create Deployment Imperatively

```bash
kubectl create deployment my-web-app-deployment \
  --image=wordpress \
  --replicas=2
```

### Step 2: Scaffold and Patch NodePort Service

Because `kubectl expose` does not offer a `--node-port` flag directly, generate the YAML draft and specify `nodePort`:

```bash
kubectl expose deployment my-web-app-deployment \
  --name=my-web-app-service \
  --type=NodePort \
  --port=80 \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml > svc.yaml
```

Add `nodePort: 30770` under `spec.ports[0]`:

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: my-web-app-service
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: my-web-app-deployment
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 80
      nodePort: 30770
      protocol: TCP
```

Apply the service:

```bash
kubectl apply -f svc.yaml
```

### Alternative: Single-Line Imperative Patch

```bash
kubectl expose deployment my-web-app-deployment \
  --name=my-web-app-service \
  --type=NodePort \
  --port=80

kubectl patch svc my-web-app-service --type='json' -p='[
  {"op": "add", "path": "/spec/ports/0/nodePort", "value": 30770}
]'
```

---

## 3. Declarative Manifest References

### Deployment Manifest (`cka-lab-my-web-app-deployment.yaml`)

```yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: my-web-app-deployment
  namespace: {{ cka_lab_networking_namespace | default('default') }}
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: my-web-app-deployment
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: my-web-app-deployment
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: wordpress
          image: wordpress:latest
          ports:
            - name: http
              containerPort: 80
```

### Service Manifest (`cka-lab-my-web-app-service.yaml`)

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: my-web-app-service
  namespace: {{ cka_lab_networking_namespace | default('default') }}
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: my-web-app-deployment
  ports:
    - name: http
      port: 80
      targetPort: 80
      nodePort: 30770
      protocol: TCP
```

---

## 4. Verification Steps

### Step 1: Check Pod Rollout and Ready Status

```bash
kubectl rollout status deployment/my-web-app-deployment
kubectl get pods -l app=my-web-app-deployment -o wide
```

### Step 2: Inspect Service and NodePort Assignment

```bash
kubectl get svc my-web-app-service
```

Expected output:

```text
NAME                 TYPE       CLUSTER-IP      EXTERNAL-IP   PORT(S)        AGE
my-web-app-service   NodePort   10.96.x.x       <none>        80:30770/TCP   10s
```

### Step 3: Verify Endpoint Registration

```bash
kubectl get endpoints my-web-app-service
```

Both Pod IPs should appear in the `ENDPOINTS` list.

### Step 4: Test Ingress on Cluster Node IPs

```bash
curl -I http://192.168.56.20:30770/
curl -I http://192.168.56.21:30770/
curl -I http://192.168.56.22:30770/
```

Returns HTTP `200 OK` or `302 Found` (redirect to WordPress setup page).

---

## 5. Automated Verification & Teardown

Run the included automated verification script:

```bash
./test-my-web-app-service.sh
```

To clean up resources created during this drill:

```bash
kubectl delete service my-web-app-service
kubectl delete deployment my-web-app-deployment
```
